\description{
Fits the same unadjusted mean-difference point estimate as
\code{\link[EDI:InferenceAllSimpleAverageDiff]{InferenceAllSimpleAverageDiff}},
\eqn{\hat\beta_T = \bar y_T - \bar y_C}, but performs inference via the
classical \strong{pooled equal-variance} Student's t-test instead of
Welch's unequal-variance version: pooled variance \eqn{s_p^2 =
\left((n_T-1)s_T^2 + (n_C-1)s_C^2\right)/(n_T+n_C-2)}, standard error
\eqn{s_p\sqrt{1/n_T + 1/n_C}}, and exact degrees of freedom \eqn{n_T+n_C-2}
— see \code{$compute_asymp_confidence_interval()} for the full formula.
This assumes the two arms have equal population variance; prefer
\code{\link[EDI:InferenceAllSimpleAverageDiff]{InferenceAllSimpleAverageDiff}}
when that assumption is doubtful, since the pooled estimator's nominal
coverage degrades under heteroskedasticity with unequal arm sizes. This
class does not support censored survival data (enforced at construction).
This class has no likelihood tier (\code{likelihood_tier = "none"}) and
provides asymptotic Wald, randomization, and bootstrap (including Bayesian
bootstrap) confidence intervals and p-values. Warm starts are disabled for
this class, since the simple mean difference is a closed-form estimator
(no iterative fit to warm-start).

Initialize simple pooled-variance mean-difference inference
  for continuous responses and prepare the pooled standard-error
  calculation used by
  \code{\link[EDI:InferenceAllSimpleMeanDiffPooledVar]{InferenceAllSimpleMeanDiffPooledVar}}.
  Disables warm starts (closed-form estimator) and asserts
  \code{des_obj} has no censored observations (unsupported by this
  class).

Computes a \eqn{1-\alpha} level confidence interval for the
  simple (unadjusted) mean-difference treatment effect
  \eqn{\hat\beta_T = \bar y_T - \bar y_C}, using the classical
  \strong{pooled equal-variance} Student's t-test formula (unlike
  \code{\link[EDI:InferenceAllSimpleAverageDiff]{InferenceAllSimpleAverageDiff}}'s
  Welch unequal-variance version): the pooled variance estimate
  \eqn{s_p^2 = \left((n_T-1)s_T^2 + (n_C-1)s_C^2\right) / (n_T+n_C-2)}
  gives standard error \eqn{\widehat{\mathrm{SE}}(\hat\beta_T) =
  s_p\sqrt{1/n_T + 1/n_C}} with exact degrees of freedom \eqn{n_T + n_C -
  2}; the interval is \eqn{\hat\beta_T \pm t_{\mathrm{df}, 1-\alpha/2}\,
  \widehat{\mathrm{SE}}(\hat\beta_T)}. Assumes equal population
  variances in the two arms — use
  \code{\link[EDI:InferenceAllSimpleAverageDiff]{InferenceAllSimpleAverageDiff}}
  instead when that assumption is doubtful. Requires at least 2
  observations per arm; otherwise returns \code{c(NA, NA)}. See
  \code{\link[EDI:InferenceAsymp]{InferenceAsymp}} for the shared
  asymptotic confidence-interval contract this participates in.

Computes a two-sided pooled-variance Student's t-test
  p-value testing \eqn{H_0: \beta_T = \code{delta}}, from the same
  pooled standard error and exact \eqn{n_T+n_C-2} degrees of freedom
  used by \code{$compute_asymp_confidence_interval()} — see that
  method's documentation for the full formula. See
  \code{\link[EDI:InferenceAsymp]{InferenceAsymp}} for the shared
  asymptotic two-sided p-value contract this participates in.
}
\section{Small-sample behaviour}{

How fast the size (and coverage) error of each procedure is expected to vanish as the
effective sample size \eqn{n_{eff}}{n_eff} grows, from EDI's convergence-rate registry
(\code{inference_convergence_expectation()}). \emph{Exact} procedures are valid at every
n by construction; the others are asymptotic, so a small-sample excess is expected, and
its constant is not given by an order. The table is the expectation for a balanced
complete-randomization design; it is not a measured size. Reference keys are explained
under References.

\tabular{lllllll}{\strong{Procedure} \tab \strong{Regime} \tab \strong{Error order} \tab \strong{Effective n} \tab \strong{Regularity} \tab \strong{Basis} \tab \strong{References} \cr
\code{rand} \tab finite sample \tab exact \tab subjects \tab regular \tab theory \tab [Fisher1935], [LehmannRomano2005] \cr
\code{wald} \tab asymptotic \tab \eqn{O(n_{eff}^{-1})}{O(n_eff^-1)} \tab subjects \tab regular \tab assumed default \tab [Hall1992] \cr
\code{jackknife} \tab asymptotic \tab \eqn{O(n_{eff}^{-1})}{O(n_eff^-1)} \tab subjects \tab regular \tab assumed default \tab [ShaoTu1995] \cr
\code{bootstrap} \tab asymptotic \tab \eqn{O(n_{eff}^{-1})}{O(n_eff^-1)} \tab subjects \tab regular \tab assumed default \tab [Hall1992] \cr
\code{bayes_boot} \tab asymptotic \tab \eqn{O(n_{eff}^{-1})}{O(n_eff^-1)} \tab subjects \tab regular \tab assumed default \tab [Rubin1981], [Lo1987] \cr
\code{rand_bootstrap} \tab asymptotic \tab \eqn{O(n_{eff}^{-1})}{O(n_eff^-1)} \tab subjects \tab regular \tab assumed default \tab [Hall1992], [Kallus2018]}

The point estimate is unbiased under randomization (theory: [Neyman1923], [ImbensRubin2015]).

Rows with basis \emph{assumed default}: the order shown is the procedure's rate in a
regular parametric model, and the cited works establish it for that case only. EDI has not
derived it for this class.

Design-specific differences from the table:
\itemize{
\item On any design with unequal allocation (\code{prob_T} not 0.5), for \code{wald}, \code{jackknife}, \code{bootstrap}, \code{bayes_boot}, \code{rand_bootstrap}: the effective n is the size of the smaller arm.
\item On \code{DesignFixedBinaryMatch}, \code{DesignFixedBlocking}, \code{DesignFixedGreedy}, \code{DesignFixedGreedyDOptimal}, \code{DesignFixedMatchingGreedyPairSwitching}, \code{DesignFixedOptimal}, \code{DesignFixedOptimalBlocks}, \code{DesignFixedRerandomization}, \code{DesignSeqOneByOneKK14}, \code{DesignSeqOneByOneKK21}, \code{DesignSeqOneByOneKK21stepwise}, \code{DesignSeqOneByOnePocockSimon}, \code{DesignSeqOneByOneSPBR}, for \code{wald}: the error order is \eqn{O(1)}{O(1)} (design mismatch; [BaiRomanoShaikh2021], [BugniCanayShaikh2018], [MaHuZhang2015], [LiDingRubin2018]).
\item On \code{DesignFixedBlockedCluster}, \code{DesignFixedCluster}, for \code{wald}, \code{jackknife}, \code{bootstrap}, \code{bayes_boot}, \code{rand_bootstrap}: the effective n is the number of clusters.
}
